<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayed Darwish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sayyid Darwish, Sheikh Sayyed Darwish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayed Darwish was an Egyptian singer and composer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, considered the father of Egyptian popular music, who set to music Bilady, Bilady, Bilady, the song that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s national anthem. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> recitation and for two years at al-Azhar, he worked as a bricklayer and café singer before turning in 1918 to musical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, composing operettas for Naguib el-Rihani's company, for Munira al-Mahdiyya and others. His songs of the 1919 revolt against British occupation, mostly with words by the poet Badi' Khayri, voiced national feeling, and light melodies such as Salma ya Salama passed into Egyptian folklore. He died in September 1923, aged thirty-one.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayed-darwish.jpg" alt="Sayed Darwish" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayed_Darwish_El_Bahr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its words were written by Muhammad Yunis al-Qady and its music composed by <b>Sayed Darwish</b>, a composer who kept close ties with early leaders of Egypt's national movement for independence, among them Mustafa Kamel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>The claim that the tune comes from &quot;Bint Misr&quot; by the Egyptian musician <b>Sayed Darwish</b> is thought to be incorrect.</blockquote><a href="/songs/misirlou">Misirlou</a></li></ul></section>
